REGIONAL REGULATION OF GARUT REGENCY

NUMBER 13 OF 2016

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

THE REGENT OF GARUT,

Considering:

a. that in order to guarantee and protect the rights of women and children so that they can participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and honor, and receive protection from violence, it is necessary to carry out efforts to protect women and children optimally through increasing broad community participation;
b. that the rampant cases of violence against women and children in Garut Regency require handling and concrete action from the Regional Government to formulate policies and programs to eliminate all forms of violence against women and children;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children from Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 8), as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by Amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times, most recently by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
6. Law Number 17 of 2003 concerning State Finance (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 47, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4286);
7. Law Number 1 of 2004 concerning State Treasury (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 5, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4374);
8. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
9. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
10. Law Number 23 of 2006 concerning Population Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 124, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4674) as amended by Law Number 24 of 2013 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2006 concerning Population Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2013 Number 232, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5475);
11. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
12. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
13. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
14. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5597) as has been amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
15. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
16. Government Regulation Number 50 of 2007 concerning Procedures for the Implementation of Regional Cooperation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 112, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4761);
17. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
18. Presidential Regulation Number 69 of 2008 concerning the Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons;
19. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 02 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
20. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 03 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
21. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2009 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons;
22. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards (SPM) in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);
23. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 42);
24. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 9 of 2011 concerning Vigilance Against the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 714);
25. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903);
26. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 08 of 2012 concerning Guidelines for Strengthening Dasawisma Groups for the Prevention and Early Handling of Acts of Violence Against Children;
27. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 10 of 2012 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Strengthening of Task Forces for the Prevention and Handling of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 984);
28. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 11 of 2012 concerning Guidelines for the Prevention and Handling of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons Based on Community and Communities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 1048);
29. Regional Regulation of Garut Regency Number 2 of 2014 concerning the Establishment of Regional Regulations (Regional Gazette of Garut Regency of 2014 Number 2, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Garut Regency Number 2);
30. Regional Regulation of Garut Regency Number 6 of 2016 concerning Concurrent Government Affairs of Garut Regency (Regional Gazette of Garut Regency of 2016 Number 6);

With Mutual Approval

THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF GARUT REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF GARUT

HAS DECIDED:

To Enact:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM VIOLENCE.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES, PURPOSE AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of women and children from violence is based on the principles of:
a. humanity;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. protection;
d. best interests of women and children; and
e. non-discrimination.

Article 4

The objectives of protecting women and children from violence are:
a. to prevent violence against women and children;
b. to eliminate all forms of violence against women and children;
c. to protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. to provide services to women and children victims of violence;
e. to carry out rehabilitation and reintegration of women and children victims of violence; and
f. to empower women and children victims of violence.

CHAPTER III

SCOPE

Article 5

The scope of protection of women and children from violence is as follows:
a. forms of violence against women and children;
b. rights of victims;
c. obligations and responsibilities;
d. implementation of protection of women and children from violence;
e. institutions;
f. cooperation and partnerships;
g. monitoring and evaluation;
h. reporting;
i. funding; and
j. Sanctions.

CHAPTER IV

FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Article 6

Forms of violence against women and children include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/orf. other violence.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 8

Women and children victims of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to assistance;
j. the right to obtain visum et repertum free of charge; and
k. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process.

Article 9

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 8, also receive special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma;
e. the right to obtain freedom; and
f. the right to be tried based on the principle of restorative justice.

Article 10

Obligations and responsibilities in implementing protection of women and children from violence are the shared responsibility of:
a. Regional Government;
b. Village Government;
c. Community; and
d. Parents, Guardians and/or Family.

Article 15

The Regional Government carries out activities for the implementation of protection of women and children from violence, which include:
a. prevention;
b. services;
c. social rehabilitation; and
d. empowerment.

Article 16

1. Prevention as referred to in Article 15 letter a, is implemented by:
a. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a violence prevention system;
d. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; and
e. providing knowledge about prevention and mechanisms for overcoming violence against women and children.
2. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented in an integrated manner by Regency Regional Apparatus and related agencies that have principal tasks and functions in the fields of:
a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. education;
c. health;
d. social affairs;
e. manpower;
f. youth and sports; and
g. mental and spiritual.
3. In addition to being implemented by Regency Regional Apparatus and related agencies as referred to in paragraph (2), prevention is also implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community, NGOs, community organizations, social organizations and religious organizations; and
c. business sector.

Article 18

Service principles as referred to in Article 17 letter a, consist of:
a. fast;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. not charged; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 19

Forms of services to victims as referred to in Article 17 letter b, consist of:
a. services to women and children victims of acts of violence, include:
1. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
2. assistance services;
3. health services;
4. legal aid services; and
5. repatriation and social reintegration services.

b. special services to women victims of acts of domestic violence or trafficking in persons, include:
1. complaint services;
2. pick-up services;
3. medical and psychological referral services;
4. shelter services;
5. assistance and legal aid services; and
6. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 21

Assistance services as referred to in Article 19 letter a number 2 include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the psychological recovery process;
c. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;
d. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
e. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
f. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by mass media;
g. coordinating with other assistants; and
h. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 25

1. Forms of services as referred to in Article 19, are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Government and implemented by Regency Regional Apparatus that have tasks and functions in the fields of:
a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. social affairs;
c. manpower;
d. health;
e. education;
f. security and order; and
g. mental and spiritual.
2. In carrying out the tasks and functions as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regional Government cooperates with the Government, Provincial Government, other Regency/City Governments, and the community.

Article 29

Improving economic enterprises as referred to in Article 27 letter b by providing:
a. job skills training;
b. productive economic enterprise training;
c. assistance in developing productive economic enterprises; and
d. ease of access to information and marketing promotion of productive economic enterprise products.

Article 31

To assist the protection of women and children, the Regional Government may establish:
a. P2TP2A at the Regional level;
b. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level;
c. Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at the Village/Sub-District Level; and
d. Other institutions or organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Part Two

P2TP2A

Article 32

1. P2TP2A as referred to in Article 31 letter a, constitutes a forum for women and children protection services that is community-based and functions as an integrated service center in providing protection to women and children from violence.
2. The membership composition of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of, among others:
a. elements of the Regional Government;
b. academics;
c. legal experts;
d. psychologists;
e. psychiatrists;
f. religious leaders; and
g. community elements.
3. The main duties of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing protection to women and children; and
b. improving the quality of life of women and children.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment of P2TP2A are stipulated by Regent's Decree.

Article 33

1. The Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in Article 31 letter b, constitutes a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Sub-District level.
2. The Management of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. elements of Sub-District apparatus;
b. agencies/institutions at the Sub-District level;
c. elements of the Sector Police (Polsek);
d. elements of the Military District Command (Koramil);
e. community leaders/religious leaders;
f. youth elements; and
g. elements of the Sub-District PKK Mobilization Team.
3. The main duties of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving community complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Sub-District and Sub-District/Village areas;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance to victims or referring to P2TP2A, Sector Police (Polsek)/Resort Police (Polres), hospitals or other institutions as required.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1), are stipulated by Sub-District Head's Decree.

Article 34

1. The Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at the Village/Sub-District Level as referred to in Article 31 letter c, constitutes a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Village/Sub-District level.
2. The Management of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at the Village/Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. elements of Village/Sub-District apparatus;
b. elements of Village Development Non-Commissioned Officer (Babinsa)/Sub-District;
c. elements of Community Development Non-Commissioned Officer (Babinmas);
d. community leaders/religious leaders;
e. youth elements; and
f. elements of the Village/Sub-District PKK Mobilization Team
3. The main duties of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at the Village/Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving community complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Village/Sub-District area;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance to victims or referring to the task force for the protection of women and children at the Sub-District level.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties and functions of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at the Village/Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1), are stipulated by Village Head/Sub-District Head's Decree.

Article 35

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children as referred to in Article 4, the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. the Government;
b. other Regional Governments; and/or
c. non-governmental institutions.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. empowerment.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in the form of a Joint Agreement and/or Cooperation Agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
